# This test checks if the postprocessor 'melt fraction' works
# as intended. It is based on the test 'melt_fraction_postprocessor.prm'.
# This version of the postprocessor aims to extend the original melt fraction
# postprocessor (tested in the above mentioned) by allowing the effect of water
# on melt fraction to be included, following Ball et al. (2022), and itself
# based on Katz et al. (2003).

# The visualization output is then also a p-T-diagram of the melt fraction
# with the temperature ranging from 1100°C to 2000°C on the x axis and the
# pressure ranging from 0 GPa to 8 GPa on the y axis.

# The data can be plotted in gnuplot using:
# set view map
# splot "file1" using 5:6:7 with points pointtype 5 pointsize 1 palette,
#       "file2" using 5:6:7 with points pointtype 5 pointsize 1 palette
# MPI: 2

include $ASPECT_SOURCE_DIR/benchmarks/melt_fraction_hydrous/melt_fraction_hydrous.prm

# The initial global refinement is set to 9 in the benchmark because resolution was
# found key to reproduce firmly e.g. Fig. 4 in Katz et al. (2003), one of the validation tests.
# A lower number also shows neat, though less accurate, results.
subsection Mesh refinement
  set Initial global refinement                = 7
end
